==Old Norse==

===Etymology 1===
From the same ultimate origin as {{term|sá|lang=non}} and {{term|þat|lang=non}}. Related to Old English {{term|se|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old English {{term|þes|lang=ang}}, Old High German {{term|dese|lang=goh}}, English {{term|this|lang=en}} and German {{term|diese|lang=de}}.

====Pronoun====
{{head|non|pronoun|cat2=demonstrative pronouns|neuter|þetta}}

# {{context|demonstrative|lang=is}} [[this]], [[that]] (referring to both persons and things)

=====Declension=====
{{non-decl-pron-sjá}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|non}} {{m|gem-pro|*sehwaną||to see}} (for cognates see there). Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|non}} {{m|ine-pro|*sekʷ-||to see, notice}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|non|séa}}
* {{l|non|siá}}

====Verb====
{{non-verb}}

# to [[see]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{rfinfl|non|verb}}
{{catlangname|non|class 5 strong verbs}}

=====Descendants=====
* Faroese: {{l|fo|síggja}}
* Icelandic: {{l|is|sjá}}
* Norwegian Nynorsk: {{l|nn|sjå}}
* Swedish: {{l|sv|se}} (from {{l|non|séa}})
* Danish: {{l|da|se}} (from {{l|non|séa}})

